# Ask before committing after each phase

When a phase in a technical PRD (`ai-workspace/*_prd.md`) is complete — its tests pass, its acceptance criteria are checked, and its status marker is updated — stop there. Do not start the next phase in the same turn.

Ask the user whether to commit and push. Do not commit or push unless they say yes.

Say which files would be committed, in one or two sentences, and suggest a commit message that says why the phase exists. Example:

> Phase 1 is done. Commit `migrations/0002_create-mcq.sql` and `migrations/create-mcq.test.ts`, then push `feature/mcq`?
>
> Suggested message: `Add the questions, choices, and attempts tables.`

If they say yes, commit only the files for that phase, using the repository's existing message style, then push the current branch with `git push`. If it has no upstream yet, use `git push -u origin HEAD`. Never push to `main` or `master`. Never force-push.

If a later request starts the next phase while the finished phase is still uncommitted, ask once whether to commit and push first, or continue without committing.
